# The REST contract. `redocly bundle` decides its layout now — 2-space, its own key order — and
# `check:contracts-bundle` asserts the committed bytes equal a fresh run. Prettier reformatting it
# afterwards would make that check fail on a file nobody edited, exactly as it would for the demo
# dataset below. The MODULE documents it is built from are formatted as normal.
openapi.yaml

# The demo dataset. `npm run seed:export` writes it by seeding a throwaway database and recording
# what the API answers, and `check:seed-export` asserts the committed bytes match what a fresh run
# produces. Prettier reformatting it afterwards makes that check fail on a file nobody edited — two
# writers, one artefact — and the copy in the paired frontend is hash-compared against this one, so
# a format-only difference would read as a forked dataset.
db/demo/demo-data.json

# Contract fragments are verbatim SLICES of a committed document, not standalone files: each one
# starts at the indentation it holds inside its parent, and several are not even parseable alone —
# half an object literal, a folder out of a YAML list, a run of JSON array entries. Prettier would
# dedent, re-quote and re-comma them, and every bundle is asserted byte-identical against its
# committed output, so formatting a fragment would corrupt the very thing it builds.
#
# The exceptions below are the point of the rule, not holes in it: `src/modules/<name>/openapi.yaml`,
# `src/modules/<name>/asyncapi.yaml`, `src/modules/<name>/analytics.ts` and the two shared asyncapi
# documents are WHOLE, valid files — a contract you can lint and open in a viewer on its own — so
# prettier formats them like any other authored source. If a format-only change ever alters a
# bundle, `check:contracts-bundle` says so.
shared/contracts/*
!shared/contracts/asyncapi.root.yaml
!shared/contracts/asyncapi.workers.yaml
